[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup (Main Line) / Study 1: White Plays the 2 Knights Setup (Nc3 + Nf3)  / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bf79f35-d2a5-41b0-b100-0e7d4b23df14/chapters/ac243e35-60d4-4aa3-b0cd-f58b6f89ff89/studies/74bdcd43-474e-42be-b72e-44d6696d7630/lines"]

1.d4 {In the Gotham Dutch course, get ready to face positions where you will be able to show your tactical ability with the Black pieces. In the Gotham Dutch, we will fianchetto our queenside bishop. In this first study, we will go over one of the most natural and popular setups that White can play: developing both knights to c3 and f3.} 1...f5 2.c4 {2.c4 is by far the most popular move. Many 1.d4 players are taught to move their d- and c-pawns together to control the center, just like 1.e4 players like playing e4 and d4. Throughout this course, we will be developing our knight on the f6-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {On the other hand, White often puts their knight on c3 (71% of the time). Further down the line, White may have ideas of d5 or e4, and this knight helps support those pawn breaks. 3...e6 helps control the d5-square to make the move d5 not so easy for White, while possibly preparing ...d5.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {The two knights is now the most popular setup, being played nearly 28% of the time, which accounts for more than 1,000,000 games! The variation I recommend here is 4...Bb4, with the following ideas: We increase the grip on e4 by pinning their one piece that controls it, make d5 harder to play for White, and damage White's structure.} 4...Bb4 5.Bg5 {The most popular move is 5.Bg5, pinning our own knight in return. It sits at 31% in the database. I suggest we move forward with our idea of damaging the pawns since White's Bg5 idea isn't all that effective.} 5...Bxc3+ 6.bxc3 {White recaptures, and we should attack the bishop here. The point is that after giving up our dark-squared bishop in the Dutch—seeing as our pawns are mostly on light squares—we would be happy if White gives up their dark-squared bishop as well.} 6...h6 7.Bxf6 {The most popular move is White simply agreeing to the trade, played 50% of the time. We're happy with our opening situation because our queen gets developed and we avoid doubling the pawns. As a result, we have a position in which our grip of the e4-square gives us some bragging rights.} 7...Qxf6 8.Qc2 {Remember how earlier I mentioned that e4 was a pawn break that White may try going for? Well, putting the queen on c2 supports this idea, so you may see this being played. Because we already have three pawns on light squares, the best square for our bishop has to be b7.} 8...b6 9.e4 {After White achieves their predictable e4-pawn push... We won't capture, letting them centralize their queen. Rather, we'll just develop.} 9...Bb7 10.exf5 {Taking on f5 does also seem enticing for White, because they are splitting our pawns. But taking on f3 is strong!} 10...Bxf3 11.gxf3 {This helps us gain yet another positional edge.} 11...exf5 {We can now just recapture on f5 and get ready to continue development. If 12.Qe2+, I like 12...Kf7, with the idea of 13...Re8. We'll go ...Nc6 after that, but we'll also castle kingside followed by ...Nc6 if White doesn't give the check on e2.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup (Main Line) / Study 1: White Plays the 2 Knights Setup (Nc3 + Nf3)  / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bf79f35-d2a5-41b0-b100-0e7d4b23df14/chapters/ac243e35-60d4-4aa3-b0cd-f58b6f89ff89/studies/74bdcd43-474e-42be-b72e-44d6696d7630/lines"]

1.d4 {In the Gotham Dutch course, get ready to face positions where you will be able to show your tactical ability with the Black pieces. In the Gotham Dutch, we will fianchetto our queenside bishop. In this first study, we will go over one of the most natural and popular setups that White can play: developing both knights to c3 and f3.} 1...f5 2.c4 {2.c4 is by far the most popular move. Many 1.d4 players are taught to move their d- and c-pawns together to control the center, just like 1.e4 players like playing e4 and d4. Throughout this course, we will be developing our knight on the f6-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {On the other hand, White often puts their knight on c3 (71% of the time). Further down the line, White may have ideas of d5 or e4, and this knight helps support those pawn breaks. 3...e6 helps control the d5-square to make the move d5 not so easy for White, while possibly preparing ...d5.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {The two knights is now the most popular setup, being played nearly 28% of the time, which accounts for more than 1,000,000 games! The variation I recommend here is 4...Bb4, with the following ideas: We increase the grip on e4 by pinning their one piece that controls it, make d5 harder to play for White, and damage White's structure.} 4...Bb4 5.Bg5 {The most popular move is 5.Bg5, pinning our own knight in return. It sits at 31% in the database. I suggest we move forward with our idea of damaging the pawns since White's Bg5 idea isn't all that effective.} 5...Bxc3+ 6.bxc3 {White recaptures, and we should attack the bishop here. The point is that after giving up our dark-squared bishop in the Dutch—seeing as our pawns are mostly on light squares—we would be happy if White gives up their dark-squared bishop as well.} 6...h6 7.Bxf6 {The most popular move is White simply agreeing to the trade, played 50% of the time. We're happy with our opening situation because our queen gets developed and we avoid doubling the pawns. As a result, we have a position in which our grip of the e4-square gives us some bragging rights.} 7...Qxf6 8.Qc2 {Remember how earlier I mentioned that e4 was a pawn break that White may try going for? Well, putting the queen on c2 supports this idea, so you may see this being played. Because we already have three pawns on light squares, the best square for our bishop has to be b7.} 8...b6 9.e4 {After White achieves their predictable e4-pawn push... We won't capture, letting them centralize their queen. Rather, we'll just develop.} 9...Bb7 10.e5 {Many players like gaining space. But space doesn't always constitute a better position. The queen on f7 helps control the d5-square. Even though it looks like it doesn't have much scope, it's deceptively active.} 10...Qf7 11.Be2 {The bishop can be developed to e2. White's best idea is just to castle since they don't have any good pawn moves to make. I suggest we castle as well.} 11...O-O 12.O-O {White castles...} 12...d6 {This is why I had said that having space isn't always being 100% better. The advanced pawn can be undermined! We'll continue development with ...Nd7 and ...Kh8, just to be extra safe with our king in case something could get on its same diagonal. After exd6 cxd6, we'll also have a semi-open c-file to place a rook on with moves like ...Rac8.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup (Main Line) / Study 1: White Plays the 2 Knights Setup (Nc3 + Nf3)  / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bf79f35-d2a5-41b0-b100-0e7d4b23df14/chapters/ac243e35-60d4-4aa3-b0cd-f58b6f89ff89/studies/74bdcd43-474e-42be-b72e-44d6696d7630/lines"]

1.d4 {In the Gotham Dutch course, get ready to face positions where you will be able to show your tactical ability with the Black pieces. In the Gotham Dutch, we will fianchetto our queenside bishop. In this first study, we will go over one of the most natural and popular setups that White can play: developing both knights to c3 and f3.} 1...f5 2.c4 {2.c4 is by far the most popular move. Many 1.d4 players are taught to move their d- and c-pawns together to control the center, just like 1.e4 players like playing e4 and d4. Throughout this course, we will be developing our knight on the f6-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {On the other hand, White often puts their knight on c3 (71% of the time). Further down the line, White may have ideas of d5 or e4, and this knight helps support those pawn breaks. 3...e6 helps control the d5-square to make the move d5 not so easy for White, while possibly preparing ...d5.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {The two knights is now the most popular setup, being played nearly 28% of the time, which accounts for more than 1,000,000 games! The variation I recommend here is 4...Bb4, with the following ideas: We increase the grip on e4 by pinning their one piece that controls it, make d5 harder to play for White, and damage White's structure.} 4...Bb4 5.Bg5 {The most popular move is 5.Bg5, pinning our own knight in return. It sits at 31% in the database. I suggest we move forward with our idea of damaging the pawns since White's Bg5 idea isn't all that effective.} 5...Bxc3+ 6.bxc3 {White recaptures, and we should attack the bishop here. The point is that after giving up our dark-squared bishop in the Dutch—seeing as our pawns are mostly on light squares—we would be happy if White gives up their dark-squared bishop as well.} 6...h6 7.Bxf6 {The most popular move is White simply agreeing to the trade, played 50% of the time. We're happy with our opening situation because our queen gets developed and we avoid doubling the pawns. As a result, we have a position in which our grip of the e4-square gives us some bragging rights.} 7...Qxf6 8.e3 {White could just be solid and play e3 to develop their light-squared bishop. We can castle here. Although White has their pawns on dark squares, our pawns are on light squares, which helps fight the influence of White's light-squared bishop. Once again: control of e4 is what we're aiming for here in the Dutch.} 8...O-O 9.Bd3 {In d4 openings, the d3-square is often seen as the optimal square for White's light-squared bishop. However, against the Dutch, this doesn't exactly work because the Dutch f-pawn blocks the diagonal. This is the most popular move here, sitting at 62% in this position. Once again, thanks to our Dutch pawn formation, b7 will be the best square for the bishop.} 9...b6 10.O-O {Castling is now the most popular move, played around 65% of the time. White is just developing. And now we can get our bishop to b7 and control the e4-square even further.} 10...Bb7 11.Qc2 {11.Qc2 is logical as it contributes to the e4-push. Against it, we have ideas of ...Bxf3. These ideas are possible when the queen moves away from d1 at other points in this opening, not only at move 11.} 11...Bxf3 12.gxf3 {White recaptures, with a structural disadvantage now.} 12...Nc6 {And we won't have any problem at all in achieving the ...e5-push. ...Re8 and ...Kh8 are some improving moves that you should consider depending on how the game progresses. In general, you'll have better prospects because of the pawn structure. You could even consider ...g5 at some point, but it's useful to prepare that move with ...Kh8.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup (Main Line) / Study 1: White Plays the 2 Knights Setup (Nc3 + Nf3)  / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bf79f35-d2a5-41b0-b100-0e7d4b23df14/chapters/ac243e35-60d4-4aa3-b0cd-f58b6f89ff89/studies/74bdcd43-474e-42be-b72e-44d6696d7630/lines"]

1.d4 {In the Gotham Dutch course, get ready to face positions where you will be able to show your tactical ability with the Black pieces. In the Gotham Dutch, we will fianchetto our queenside bishop. In this first study, we will go over one of the most natural and popular setups that White can play: developing both knights to c3 and f3.} 1...f5 2.c4 {2.c4 is by far the most popular move. Many 1.d4 players are taught to move their d- and c-pawns together to control the center, just like 1.e4 players like playing e4 and d4. Throughout this course, we will be developing our knight on the f6-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {On the other hand, White often puts their knight on c3 (71% of the time). Further down the line, White may have ideas of d5 or e4, and this knight helps support those pawn breaks. 3...e6 helps control the d5-square to make the move d5 not so easy for White, while possibly preparing ...d5.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {The two knights is now the most popular setup, being played nearly 28% of the time, which accounts for more than 1,000,000 games! The variation I recommend here is 4...Bb4, with the following ideas: We increase the grip on e4 by pinning their one piece that controls it, make d5 harder to play for White, and damage White's structure.} 4...Bb4 5.Bg5 {The most popular move is 5.Bg5, pinning our own knight in return. It sits at 31% in the database. I suggest we move forward with our idea of damaging the pawns since White's Bg5 idea isn't all that effective.} 5...Bxc3+ 6.bxc3 {White recaptures, and we should attack the bishop here. The point is that after giving up our dark-squared bishop in the Dutch—seeing as our pawns are mostly on light squares—we would be happy if White gives up their dark-squared bishop as well.} 6...h6 7.Bxf6 {The most popular move is White simply agreeing to the trade, played 50% of the time. We're happy with our opening situation because our queen gets developed and we avoid doubling the pawns. As a result, we have a position in which our grip of the e4-square gives us some bragging rights.} 7...Qxf6 8.e3 {White could just be solid and play e3 to develop their light-squared bishop. We can castle here. Although White has their pawns on dark squares, our pawns are on light squares, which helps fight the influence of White's light-squared bishop. Once again: control of e4 is what we're aiming for here in the Dutch.} 8...O-O 9.Bd3 {In d4 openings, the d3-square is often seen as the optimal square for White's light-squared bishop. However, against the Dutch, this doesn't exactly work because the Dutch f-pawn blocks the diagonal. This is the most popular move here, sitting at 62% in this position. Once again, thanks to our Dutch pawn formation, b7 will be the best square for the bishop.} 9...b6 10.Qc2 {Qc2 is an idea that certainly looks and feels natural for d4 players. In this case, it's played around 140 times in the database, sitting at around 11%. We'll get our bishop on the long diagonal.} 10...Bb7 11.e4 {11.e4 is a mistake here, played too early. We have a double attack using an x-ray on the e4-pawn!} 11...Qg6 12.exf5 {If White tries trading one of the pawns to get out of their troubles...} 12...Qxg2 {We've got another double attack. Black is completely winning. The move e4 cannot be played so readily, especially with g2 hanging.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup (Main Line) / Study 1: White Plays the 2 Knights Setup (Nc3 + Nf3)  / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bf79f35-d2a5-41b0-b100-0e7d4b23df14/chapters/ac243e35-60d4-4aa3-b0cd-f58b6f89ff89/studies/74bdcd43-474e-42be-b72e-44d6696d7630/lines"]

1.d4 {In the Gotham Dutch course, get ready to face positions where you will be able to show your tactical ability with the Black pieces. In the Gotham Dutch, we will fianchetto our queenside bishop. In this first study, we will go over one of the most natural and popular setups that White can play: developing both knights to c3 and f3.} 1...f5 2.c4 {2.c4 is by far the most popular move. Many 1.d4 players are taught to move their d- and c-pawns together to control the center, just like 1.e4 players like playing e4 and d4. Throughout this course, we will be developing our knight on the f6-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {On the other hand, White often puts their knight on c3 (71% of the time). Further down the line, White may have ideas of d5 or e4, and this knight helps support those pawn breaks. 3...e6 helps control the d5-square to make the move d5 not so easy for White, while possibly preparing ...d5.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {The two knights is now the most popular setup, being played nearly 28% of the time, which accounts for more than 1,000,000 games! The variation I recommend here is 4...Bb4, with the following ideas: We increase the grip on e4 by pinning their one piece that controls it, make d5 harder to play for White, and damage White's structure.} 4...Bb4 5.Bg5 {The most popular move is 5.Bg5, pinning our own knight in return. It sits at 31% in the database. I suggest we move forward with our idea of damaging the pawns since White's Bg5 idea isn't all that effective.} 5...Bxc3+ 6.bxc3 {White recaptures, and we should attack the bishop here. The point is that after giving up our dark-squared bishop in the Dutch—seeing as our pawns are mostly on light squares—we would be happy if White gives up their dark-squared bishop as well.} 6...h6 7.Bxf6 {The most popular move is White simply agreeing to the trade, played 50% of the time. We're happy with our opening situation because our queen gets developed and we avoid doubling the pawns. As a result, we have a position in which our grip of the e4-square gives us some bragging rights.} 7...Qxf6 8.e3 {White could just be solid and play e3 to develop their light-squared bishop. We can castle here. Although White has their pawns on dark squares, our pawns are on light squares, which helps fight the influence of White's light-squared bishop. Once again: control of e4 is what we're aiming for here in the Dutch.} 8...O-O 9.Be2 {The more modest Be2 is also possible, but this shouldn't be at all challenging. It doesn't fight for control of e4. We can go for our fianchetto.} 9...b6 10.O-O {White castles. We get our bishop on the long diagonal. It's worth noting that if you see d5 in some instances, like in this one, you can just push ...e5 and you'll have pawns that are more dangerous.} 10...Bb7 11.a4 {If White attempts to get their pawn rolling and expand on the queenside...} 11...Nc6 {We can control that square. If 12.d5, 12...Na5 and White's central pawn play is dubious. At any rate, even if d5 isn't played, we'll want to play something like ...Na5 and ...d6 to continue improving our position, controlling central squares, and having our knight not block the bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup (Main Line) / Study 1: White Plays the 2 Knights Setup (Nc3 + Nf3)  / Variation 1"]
