1.d4 {In the Gotham Dutch course, get ready to face positions where you will be able to show your tactical ability with the Black pieces. In the Gotham Dutch, we will fianchetto our queenside bishop. In this first study, we will go over one of the most natural and popular setups that White can play: developing both knights to c3 and f3.} 1...f5 2.c4 {2.c4 is by far the most popular move. Many 1.d4 players are taught to move their d- and c-pawns together to control the center, just like 1.e4 players like playing e4 and d4. Throughout this course, we will be developing our knight on the f6-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {On the other hand, White often puts their knight on c3 (71% of the time). Further down the line, White may have ideas of d5 or e4, and this knight helps support those pawn breaks. 3...e6 helps control the d5-square to make the move d5 not so easy for White, while possibly preparing ...d5.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {The two knights is now the most popular setup, being played nearly 28% of the time, which accounts for more than 1,000,000 games! The variation I recommend here is 4...Bb4, with the following ideas: We increase the grip on e4 by pinning their one piece that controls it, make d5 harder to play for White, and damage White's structure.} 4...Bb4 5.Bg5 {The most popular move is 5.Bg5, pinning our own knight in return. It sits at 31% in the database. I suggest we move forward with our idea of damaging the pawns since White's Bg5 idea isn't all that effective.} 5...Bxc3+ 6.bxc3 {White recaptures, and we should attack the bishop here. The point is that after giving up our dark-squared bishop in the Dutch—seeing as our pawns are mostly on light squares—we would be happy if White gives up their dark-squared bishop as well.} 6...h6 7.Bxf6 {The most popular move is White simply agreeing to the trade, played 50% of the time. We're happy with our opening situation because our queen gets developed and we avoid doubling the pawns. As a result, we have a position in which our grip of the e4-square gives us some bragging rights.} 7...Qxf6 8.e3 {White could just be solid and play e3 to develop their light-squared bishop. We can castle here. Although White has their pawns on dark squares, our pawns are on light squares, which helps fight the influence of White's light-squared bishop. Once again: control of e4 is what we're aiming for here in the Dutch.} 8...O-O 9.Bd3 {In d4 openings, the d3-square is often seen as the optimal square for White's light-squared bishop. However, against the Dutch, this doesn't exactly work because the Dutch f-pawn blocks the diagonal. This is the most popular move here, sitting at 62% in this position. Once again, thanks to our Dutch pawn formation, b7 will be the best square for the bishop.} 9...b6 10.O-O {Castling is now the most popular move, played around 65% of the time. White is just developing. And now we can get our bishop to b7 and control the e4-square even further.} 10...Bb7 11.Re1 {11.Re1 seeks to control the center and support the e4-pawn push at some point.} 11...Be4 {But here I like the strong 11...Be4, stopping White's e4 idea. White is advised not to take on e4 because of the x-ray on the f2-pawn. We'll go ...d6 and ...Nd7. When the time is right, maybe even ...e5 could be played. Black has strong prospects and can even go for a kingside attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup (Main Line) / Study 1: White Plays the 2 Knights Setup (Nc3 + Nf3)  / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bf79f35-d2a5-41b0-b100-0e7d4b23df14/chapters/ac243e35-60d4-4aa3-b0cd-f58b6f89ff89/studies/74bdcd43-474e-42be-b72e-44d6696d7630/lines"]

1.d4 {In the Gotham Dutch course, get ready to face positions where you will be able to show your tactical ability with the Black pieces. In the Gotham Dutch, we will fianchetto our queenside bishop. In this first study, we will go over one of the most natural and popular setups that White can play: developing both knights to c3 and f3.} 1...f5 2.c4 {2.c4 is by far the most popular move. Many 1.d4 players are taught to move their d- and c-pawns together to control the center, just like 1.e4 players like playing e4 and d4. Throughout this course, we will be developing our knight on the f6-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {On the other hand, White often puts their knight on c3 (71% of the time). Further down the line, White may have ideas of d5 or e4, and this knight helps support those pawn breaks. 3...e6 helps control the d5-square to make the move d5 not so easy for White, while possibly preparing ...d5.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {The two knights is now the most popular setup, being played nearly 28% of the time, which accounts for more than 1,000,000 games! The variation I recommend here is 4...Bb4, with the following ideas: We increase the grip on e4 by pinning their one piece that controls it, make d5 harder to play for White, and damage White's structure.} 4...Bb4 5.Bg5 {The most popular move is 5.Bg5, pinning our own knight in return. It sits at 31% in the database. I suggest we move forward with our idea of damaging the pawns since White's Bg5 idea isn't all that effective.} 5...Bxc3+ 6.bxc3 {White recaptures, and we should attack the bishop here. The point is that after giving up our dark-squared bishop in the Dutch—seeing as our pawns are mostly on light squares—we would be happy if White gives up their dark-squared bishop as well.} 6...h6 7.Bh4 {7.Bh4 is played 30% of the times, probably because people just don't see the danger. We can simply win the bishop.} 7...g5 8.Nxg5 {White could try to get two pawns for the piece and a pin on the f6-knight. We should take the piece.} 8...hxg5 9.Bxg5 {White gets their pin. But 9...Qe7 is a great defensive resource to simply consolidate this material advantage and get rid of any counterplay.} 9...Qe7 10.e3 {White continues to develop. We unpin and attack the bishop.} 10...Qg7 11.h4 {White defends.} 11...Ne4 {But White has no compensation for the piece. This should be an easy win.} *

